<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhimmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhimmi is the status under Islamic law of protected non-Muslim communities, mainly Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, who were permitted to practise their religion in return for paying a poll tax (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>) and accepting certain restrictions. It structured relations between rulers and subject populations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, where non-Muslims long formed the majority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhimmi.jpg" alt="Dhimmi" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mdktb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ALFiqh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christians and Jews were protected as <b>dhimmis</b>, paying a poll tax and keeping their own communal leaders and judges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The treatment of conquered non-Muslim populations as protected peoples (<b>dhimmis</b>), who were guaranteed security of life, property and worship in return for paying the jizya and accepting certain legal disabilities, established a model of regulated religious pluralism that, despite its inequalities, allowed the relatively rapid stabilisation of conquered…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The jizya poll tax on non-Muslim subjects (<b>dhimmis</b>) and the kharaj land tax were the main sources of revenue, which created a structural tension: conversion reduced the population liable to jizya, and some governors, notably al-Hajjaj in Iraq, continued to tax converts or sent them back to the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Conversion among the conquered peoples was gradual: Christians, Jews and Zoroastrians were permitted to keep their religions as protected peoples, the <b>dhimmis</b>, paying a special tax, and it took several centuries before Muslims formed the majority in Egypt, Syria, Iraq and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars argue that changing the status of non-Muslims from <b>dhimmi</b>, a subjugated population, to equal subjects clashed with the traditional values of the Muslim population and was in part responsible for the Hamidian massacres and the later Armenian genocide.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Abbasid Caliphate Islamic law set Muslims above the protected non-Muslims, the <b>dhimmis</b>, who paid a poll tax, and both above the enslaved.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li></ul></section>
